From 837d5c44d682798ae1afb9a04fb1ed48a04ee181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Spencer Bull <spencer@omarchy.org>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 02:15:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: set eDP 1.5 OLED panels' brightness as a target
 luminance

nvKmsSetBacklight() only asks RM to set the brightness, and RM leaves it
to the GPU firmware, which sets an internal eDP panel's brightness as a
PWM signal or a level in the VESA eDP backlight registers. The OLED
panel of the Dell XPS 16 (NVIDIA N1x, DX16263) has no PWM input and
ignores that level: the firmware switches it to DPCD mode and writes the
level, and the panel stays as it is. The brightness keys moved the
backlight device and the OSD, and nothing else.

The panel is eDP 1.5 and takes a target luminance in nits. For internal
eDP 1.5 panels without a PWM input that support luminance control, set
the brightness that way after RM has, as the kernel's drm_edp_backlight
helpers do for i915 and amdgpu: luminance control on, target 20 to 400
nits. Read it back from the panel for actual_brightness, and set it
again after a modeset brings the panel back up, since the panel forgets
it when powered off. Panels that take a PWM signal or only a level are
left to the firmware.
